WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.520
This is the Singularity Daily, for Saturday, November 7.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.300
Let's catch you up on the last 24 hours in superintelligence.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.140
Thursday, tech stocks had their worst day in 19 months.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.700
And still, nobody's slowing down.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.680
OpenAI's finance chief isn't flinching: "The demand for AI is real."

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.390
An outside lab spent 11 weeks inside Anthropic.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:39.420
Its verdict? Progress is running about twice as fast.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.690
And most of that speed-up, they think, is AI helping to build the next AI.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.840
Mira Murati's new model goes open next week,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.820
as the top-scoring open model outside China.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:53.420
Still,

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:59.350
China owns that space: its models get 89% of business spending on open models.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.050
President Trump posted that he's 100% in favour of superintelligence.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:06.660
Saying no? "Foolish."

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.290
Meanwhile, Democrats, about to run Congress,

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.720
are planning a flood of bills on AI and data centres.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.970
And Thinking Machines is reportedly raising $5 billion,

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:20.990
at a $40 billion valuation.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:28.730
Elon Musk lost $113 billion, on paper. By Friday, stocks clawed some back.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:30.690
In science,

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:35.180
a start-up says its AI cracked an 18-year-old quantum computing puzzle.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.360
And Claude helped map a bacterial version of a s

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.230
tructure that's baffled biologists for 40 years.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.210
The catch? Told to fix page numbers, an AI deleted a professor's syllabus.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:56.370
In Amsterdam, about 100 protesters stormed a Microsoft data centre site.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.370
So... is it so over?

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.450
We're so back!

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:05.640
Now, the machines. Tesla's ending the Cybertruck next year,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.620
to build its robotaxi instead.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.050
Its next AI chip promises 80 times today's computing power.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.290
Another's being designed for space.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.880
And a humanoid robot cleaned a reporter's apartment,

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.090
climbing the stairs like it was drunk.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.750
A terrible day doesn't stop progress. Don't let it stop you, either. Keep going.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.030
Day after day, the curve keeps bending upward.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.970
Every day, the future shows up a little earlier.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.930
All of that, in one day. And the days keep getting bigger.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.100
This has been the Singularity Daily.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.340
Go build something. See you tomorrow.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.010
Want the whole month? Hear more on November 27.
